e member names exist. If they don't, ImportError at collection \u2192 test fails. Risky.\n\nUsing `post()` is the safest for constructing postings. The remaining risk is only the sign convention in balance assertions.\n\nLet me try to recall pypara more concretely. pypara is by \"Onur Rauf Soy\"? Hmm... Actually I remember: pypara \u2014 \"Double-entry accounting for humans\" \u2014 GitHub: https://github.com/pypara/pypara. The journaling.py:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nThis module provides classes for the journaling model.\n\"\"\"\nimport datetime\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom enum import Enum, unique\nfrom typing import Generic, Iterable, List, NewType, Optional, TypeVar\n\nfrom pypara.accounting.accounts import Account\nfrom pypara.commons.numbers import Amount, Quantity, ZERO\nfrom pypara.commons.others import Guid, makeguid\n\n_T = TypeVar(\"_T\")\n\n\n@unique\nclass Direction(Enum):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an enumeration of posting directions.\n    \"\"\"\n    INC = 1\n    DEC = 2\n```\n\nHmm, and:\n\n```python\n    @property\n    def is_debit(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Indicates if this posting is a debit.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.direction == Direction.INC\n```\n\nHmm wait, actually now I really do recall that in pypara, `Direction` has `INC` and `DEC`, and `Posting.is_debit` is `self.direction == Direction.INC`. And `JournalEntry.post`:\n\n```python\n    def post(self, date: datetime.date, account: Account, quantity: Quantity) -> \"JournalEntry[_T]\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a new posting to this journal entry.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.postings.append(Posting(self, date, account, Direction.INC if quantity >= ZERO else Direction.DEC, quantity))\n        return self\n```\n\nAnd ledger.py:\n\n```python\n    def add(self, posting: Posting[_T]) -> LedgerEntry[_T]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a posting to this ledger.\n        \"\"\"\n        balance: Balance = Balance(posting.date, self._last_balance + posting.quantity if posting.is_debit else self._last_balance - posting.quantity)\n        entry: LedgerEntry[_T] = LedgerEntry(self, posting.journal, posting.date, posting.direction, posting.quantity, balance)\n        self.entries.append(entry)\n        return entry\n```\n\nHmm, but get_info says `Posting` has `amount: Amount`, not `quantity`. So maybe in this version:\n\n```python\n        balance: Balance = Balance(posting.date, self._last_balance + posting.amount if posting.is_debit else self._last_balance - posting.amount)\n```\n\nEither way, positive \u2192 increase (given is_debit == INC).\n\nHmm, wait, actually \u2014 I want to double-check `is_debit`. In double-entry semantics with pypara's \"INC/DEC\" naming: INC = increment, DEC = decrement. For an asset account (normal debit balance), a debit increments. So INC \u2194 debit. `is_debit == (direction == INC)`. That's consistent.\n\nBut hmm, there's another possibility: pypara's `Direction` might be `DBT`/`CDT`:\n\n```python\nclass Direction(Enum):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an enumeration of posting directions.\n    \"\"\"\n    DBT = 1\n    CDT = 2\n```\n\nHmm! Actually now I recall... In pypara, I think it IS `INC`/`DEC`. Let me think about `JournalEntry.debits` property:\n\n```python\n    @property\n    def debits(self) -> Iterable[Posting[_T]]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Returns the debit postings of this journal entry.\n        \"\"\"\n        return (p for p in self.postings if p.is_debit)\n```\n\nOK regardless \u2014 my test uses `post()` with positive quantities, and asserts balances increase. If the actual convention is opposite, the test fails. To hedge, could I make assertions that work either way? For example, assert `entries[-1].balance.value in (expected_plus, expected_minus)`? That's hacky but robust... Actually, you know what \u2014 that's not unreasonable for a coverage-focused test, but it looks weird.\n\nAlternative: assert on counts and structural properties only, plus assert that the balance changed from initial in the expected magnitude: `abs(entries[-1].balance.value - initial) == sum`? Hmm, that works regardless of sign! `abs(1425 - 1000) == 425` and `abs(575 - 1000) == 425`. But for the zero-initial ledgers: `abs(250 - 0) == 250` either way. Hmm, but that's a weaker assertion.\n\nHmm, let me think about which is more likely correct. I'm going to go with my strong recollection: pypara uses INC/DEC and positive quantities post as INC (debit), and `Ledger.add` adds for debits. Actually...\n\nHold on, let me reconsider. I found it. I'm now fairly sure about pypara's actual code. From https://github.com/pypara/pypara/blob/master/pypara/accounting/journaling.py (as I remember it):\n\n```python\n@unique\nclass Direction(Enum):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an enumeration of posting directions.\n    \"\"\"\n    INC = 1\n    DEC = 2\n```\n\nHmm, no wait. I actually recall reading pypara's code at some point. Let me reconstruct from the `Posting` docstring... \n\nOK, decision time. I'll go with positive \u2192 increase. It's the natural reading of \"INC if quantity >= ZERO\" + \"add if is_debit\" + \"INC is debit\". If I'm wrong on one of those three links, the balance assertions fail. To reduce blast radius, I could structure assertions so that the most critical coverage-related assertions (ledger existence, counts, initial balances) are independent of sign, and balance-value assertions are the only sign-dependent ones.\n\nActually \u2014 here's a cleaner hedge: use `Quantity` values and compute expected final balance as `initial + sum` but ALSO... no. Let me just also assert the count-based postconditions strongly, and include the balance assertions. If the sign convention is opposite, the test would fail \u2014 but I judge the probability low (~10-15%).\n\nHmm, alternatively, I can make the test self-calibrating: post a single known quantity to a fresh zero-balance ledger via `build_general_ledger`, then check `abs(value) == quantity`... no wait, that's what I'd assert anyway.\n\nYou know what, let me think about this differently. What does `Ledger.add` semantically do in pypara? Looking at the actual pypara repo (I'm now quite sure I've seen this code):\n\npypara/accounting/ledger.py:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass Ledger(Generic[_T]):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an account ledger model.\n    \"\"\"\n    account: Account\n    initial: Balance\n    entries: List[LedgerEntry[_T]] = field(default_factory=list, init=False)\n\n    @property\n    def last_entry(self) -> Optional[LedgerEntry[_T]]:\n        ...\n\n    @property\n    def _last_balance(self) -> Quantity:\n        \"\"\"\n        Returns the last balance of this ledger.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.entries[-1].balance.value if self.entries else self.initial.value\n\n    def add(self, posting: Posting[_T]) -> LedgerEntry[_T]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a posting to this ledger.\n        \"\"\"\n        balance: Balance = Balance(posting.date, self._last_balance + posting.quantity if posting.is_debit else self._last_balance - posting.quantity)\n        entry: LedgerEntry[_T] = LedgerEntry(self, posting.journal, posting.date, posting.direction, posting.quantity, balance)\n        self.entries.append(entry)\n        return entry\n```\n\nHmm OK wait, but `Posting` has `amount` per get_info, not `quantity`. So this version uses `posting.amount`. Fine.\n\nAnd journaling.py `post`:\n\n```python\n    def post(self, date: datetime.date, account: Account, quantity: Quantity) -> \"JournalEntry[_T]\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a new posting to this journal entry.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.postings.append(Posting(self, date, account, Direction.INC if quantity >= ZERO else Direction.DEC, quantity))\n        return self\n```\n\nHmm, but if `Posting` takes `amount`, then `post` passes `quantity` as `amount`. Fine.\n\nAnd `is_debit`:\n\n```python\n    @property\n    def is_debit(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Indicates if this post