ry].initial == Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))\n\n    ## In-period postings are appended to the ledgers of their accounts, in order,\n    ## with running balances; out-of-period postings are not:\n    cash_ledger = gl.ledgers[cash]\n    assert len(cash_ledger.entries) == 3\n    assert cash_ledger.entries[-1].balance.date == until\n    assert cash_ledger.entries[-1].balance.value == Decimal(\"1000\") + Decimal(\"250\") + Decimal(\"75\") + Decimal(\"100\")\n\n    sales_ledger = gl.ledgers[sales]\n    assert len(sales_ledger.entries) == 1\n    assert sales_ledger.entries[-1].balance.value == Decimal(\"250\")\n\n    inventory_ledger = gl.ledgers[inventory]\n    assert len(inventory_ledger.entries) == 1\n    assert inventory_ledger.entries[-1].balance.value == Decimal(\"40\")\n```\n\nHmm wait, there's one thing I should double check: `cash_ledger.entries[-1].balance.date == until`. The last cash posting is from `je_on_until`, posted with date `until`. `Ledger.add` creates `Balance(posting.date, ...)`. `posting.date` \u2014 when `post()` creates the Posting, it passes `date` (the argument to post), which is `until`. Good.\n\nBut hmm \u2014 what if `post()` uses the journal's date instead of the passed date? The signature takes `date` explicitly, so it uses it. Good.\n\nAnother check: `sales_ledger.entries[-1].balance.value == Decimal(\"250\")` \u2014 sales ledger initial is `Balance(since, Decimal(0))`, `_last_balance` \u2192 `Decimal(0)` (initial.value since no entries). First posting +250 \u2192 `Decimal(0) + Decimal(\"250\")` = `Decimal(\"250\")`. Good.\n\nWait, `Decimal(0) + Decimal(\"250\")` = `Decimal(\"250\")`. Equality with `Decimal(\"250\")` \u2192 True. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 one more paranoid check on `Balance` equality: `Balance` is `@dataclass(frozen=True)` \u2192 `__eq__` compares `(date, value)`. `initial[cash]` is `Balance(since, Decimal(\"1000\"))`; `gl.ledgers[cash].initial` is the same object actually (passed through). Equality trivially true. Good.\n