ne 183)? Yes \u2014 cash is in initial and has postings; `len(cash_ledger.entries) == 3` proves postings were added to the initial-balance ledger (not a fresh zero one). Good \u2014 that covers the false branch of the `if`.\n\nAnd sales/inventory cover the true branch (line 180).\n\nLine 174: covered by both tests (non-empty and empty initial).\nLine 177: covered (both with and without postings passing the filter).\nLine 179-180: covered (true branch).\nLine 183: covered.\nLine 186: covered.\n\nAll lines 174-186 covered. \n\nOne more thought: should I worry that `build_general_ledger` is in `pypara.accounting.ledger` but maybe `GeneralLedger`/`Ledger` need to be imported from there \u2014 yes, as discussed.\n\nAlso, is there any chance the module `pypara.accounting.ledger` imports fail due to missing dependencies? Not my concern.\n\nLet me also double-check `Quantity(Decimal(0))` vs `Decimal(0)`: `Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))` \u2014 `Quantity` is NewType, calling it returns the Decimal unchanged. The auto-created initial in the code is `Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))` \u2192 `Balance(since, Decimal(0))`. My expected: `Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))` \u2192 `Balance(since, Decimal(0))`. Equal. Good.\n\nHmm, `Decimal(0)` vs `Decimal(\"0\")` \u2014 equal values, and dataclass equality uses `==`, so `Decimal(0) == Decimal(\"0\")` \u2192 True. Good.\n\nNow, one subtle thing: in `test_build_general_ledger_empty_journal_and_initials`, passing `[]` as journal \u2014 the generator expression `(p for j in journal for p in j.postings if ...)` over empty list yields nothing. Good.\n\nLet me also consider adding a test where an account has an initial balance AND the journal is a generator (not list) \u2014 not needed.\n\nI think two tests suffice. Let me also consider: do I need `pytest` import for anything? No. But some style guides want it... I'll omit it. Actually, hmm \u2014 the instructions say \"Respond ONLY with the Python code enclosed in backticks\". So just the code block.\n\nWait, one more risk check: `MockAccount.type` property returning None \u2014 if `Ledger` or something calls `account.type`... it doesn't in the shown code. Fine.\n\nAlso, `code` property returns str \u2014 `Code` is probably a NewType of str. Fine.\n\nLet me also double check the `set(gl.ledgers)` assertion: `gl.ledgers` is `Dict[Account, Ledger]`. `set(gl.ledgers)` gives keys. `{cash, sales, inventory, equity}` \u2014 set of instances. Equality by identity \u2014 same objects. Good.\n\nOne more: `assert rent not in gl.ledgers` \u2014 `in` on dict checks keys via hash/eq \u2014 identity hash. Good.\n\nAlso, hmm \u2014 should the `je_before`/`je_after` entries be validated? No, we never call validate.\n\nPotential issue: `JournalEntry` frozen dataclass with `postings` list \u2014 when I create `je_before` and post to it, then `build_general_ledger` iterates `j.postings` \u2014 fine.\n\nEdge: the generator expression filters on `j.date` \u2014 je_before.date = 2020-12-31 < since \u2192 excluded. je_after.date = 2022-01-01 > until \u2192 excluded. Good.\n\nNow, let me reconsider the balance arithmetic one final time. `Ledger.add`:\n\n```python\nbalance = Balance(posting.date, self._last_balance + posting.amount if posting.is_debit else self._last_balance - posting.amount)\n```\n\nNote operator precedence: `self._last_balance + posting.amount if posting.is_debit else self._last_balance - posting.amount` parses as `(self._last_balance + posting.amount) if posting.is_debit else (self._last_balance - posting.amount)`. Good.\n\n`is_debit` for INC \u2192 True \u2192 adds. So positive quantities increase balance. My assertions align.\n\nBut wait \u2014 what if `Direction.INC` maps to credit and `is_debit` is `direction == Direction.DEC`? Then positive quantity \u2192 INC \u2192 is_debit False \u2192 subtract. Then cash final = 1000 - 250 - 75 - 100 = 575, and my assertion fails. Hmm. Which is it in pypara?\n\nLet me think hard about pypara's journaling.py. From the actual pypara repository (github.com/pypara/pypara? or \"onur\"? I believe it's https://github.com/pypara/pypara \u2014 hmm, actually I think the repo is \"pypara\" by \"kxseven\"? No...).\n\nOK, pypara on PyPI: \"pypara 0.0.25\" or similar. Author: \"Onur Rauf Soy\"? Hmm, I genuinely recall the GitHub repo `pypara/pypara`... Let me recall the code:\n\n```python\nclass Direction(Enum):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an enumeration of posting directions.\n    \"\"\"\n    INC = 1\n    DEC = 2\n```\n\nHmm, actually I have a vague memory that pypara's Direction is:\n\n```python\nclass Direction(Enum):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an enumeration of posting directions.\n    \"\"\"\n    INC = 1\n    DEC = 2\n```\n\nand\n\n```python\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Posting(Generic[_T]):\n    ...\n    @property\n    def is_debit(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Indicates if this posting is a debit.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.direction == Direction.INC\n\n    @property\n    def is_credit(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Indicates if this posting is a credit.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.direction == Direction.DEC\n```\n\nHmm, wait. Actually I now recall more specifically. In pypara's journaling.py:\n\n```python\nclass Direction(Enum):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an enumeration of posting directions.\n    \"\"\"\n    INC = 1\n    DEC = 2\n```\n\nHmm, but actually I have another memory that it might be:\n\n```python\nclass Direction(Enum):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an enumeration of posting directions.\n    \"\"\"\n    DEBIT = \"debit\"\n    CREDIT = \"credit\"\n```\n\nI'm not sure. But it doesn't matter for my test as long as `post()` + `Ledger.add` are consistent with each other. The question is only whether positive quantity \u2192 balance increases or decreases.\n\nIn `post`: `Direction.INC if quantity >= ZERO else Direction.DEC` (my assumption). In `Ledger.add`: `+ if is_debit else -`. If `is_debit == (direction == INC)`, posi