no wait, that's what I'd assert anyway.\n\nYou know what, let me think about this differently. What does `Ledger.add` semantically do in pypara? Looking at the actual pypara repo (I'm now quite sure I've seen this code):\n\npypara/accounting/ledger.py:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass Ledger(Generic[_T]):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an account ledger model.\n    \"\"\"\n    account: Account\n    initial: Balance\n    entries: List[LedgerEntry[_T]] = field(default_factory=list, init=False)\n\n    @property\n    def last_entry(self) -> Optional[LedgerEntry[_T]]:\n        ...\n\n    @property\n    def _last_balance(self) -> Quantity:\n        \"\"\"\n        Returns the last balance of this ledger.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.entries[-1].balance.value if self.entries else self.initial.value\n\n    def add(self, posting: Posting[_T]) -> LedgerEntry[_T]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a posting to this ledger.\n        \"\"\"\n        balance: Balance = Balance(posting.date, self._last_balance + posting.quantity if posting.is_debit else self._last_balance - posting.quantity)\n        entry: LedgerEntry[_T] = LedgerEntry(self, posting.journal, posting.date, posting.direction, posting.quantity, balance)\n        self.entries.append(entry)\n        return entry\n```\n\nHmm OK wait, but `Posting` has `amount` per get_info, not `quantity`. So this version uses `posting.amount`. Fine.\n\nAnd journaling.py `post`:\n\n```python\n    def post(self, date: datetime.date, account: Account, quantity: Quantity) -> \"JournalEntry[_T]\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a new posting to this journal entry.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.postings.append(Posting(self, date, account, Direction.INC if quantity >= ZERO else Direction.DEC, quantity))\n        return self\n```\n\nHmm, but if `Posting` takes `amount`, then `post` passes `quantity` as `amount`. Fine.\n\nAnd `is_debit`:\n\n```python\n    @property\n    def is_debit(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Indicates if this posting is a debit.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.direction == Direction.INC\n```\n\nHmm, hmm. Actually wait. I want to reconsider: maybe in pypara, `Direction` is:\n\n```python\nclass Direction(Enum):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an enumeration of posting directions.\n    \"\"\"\n    INC = 1\n    DEC = 2\n```\n\nand `is_debit` is `self.direction == Direction.INC`. I keep converging on this. Let me also sanity-check with `validate()`:\n\n```python\n    def validate(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates this journal entry.\n        \"\"\"\n        assert sum(p.amount if p.is_debit else -p.amount for p in self.postings) == 0, \"Journal entry is not balanced\"\n```\n\nSomething like that \u2014 debits and credits must offset. With INC=debit: a balanced entry has sum of INC amounts == sum of DEC amounts. OK.\n\nAlright, going with positive \u2192 increase. Final answer assertions:\n\n- cash: initial 1000, postings +250, +75, +100 \u2192 final 1425.\n- sales: 0 + 250 \u2192 250.\n- inventory: 0 + 40 \u2192 40.\n- equity: initial 5000, no postings \u2192 entries == [].\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 one more consideration on `je_mid`: it posts inventory +40 and cash +75. In real double-entry, buying inventory with cash would be inventory INC (debit) and cash DEC (credit). But for coverage purposes, direction semantics don't matter. However, if I wanted the test to be more \"realistic\", I'd use negative quantity for cash: `post(date, cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"-75\")))` \u2192 DEC \u2192 balance decreases. That would exercise the DEC path in `Ledger.add` too. But it adds sign-convention risk: if DEC subtracts (expected), cash = 1000 + 250 - 75 + 100 = 1275. If my convention assumption is wrong, it fails differently. Using all-positive keeps it simplest. I'll keep all positive.\n\nHmm, actually, wait. Let me reconsider using a negative quantity for one posting. Benefit: exercises `Direction.DEC` creation in `post` and the credit branch of `Ledger.add`. But those aren't in the uncovered lines (174-186). The uncovered lines are only in `build_general_ledger`. So no benefit for the stated goal. Keep all positive.\n\nNow \u2014 one more important check: the `if` in the generator expression: `if period.since <= j.date <= period.until`. For coverage of this line, we need the generator to be iterated \u2014 it is, by the for loop. Both branch outcomes covered (include/exclude). Good.\n\nWhat about the case where `journal` contains an entry with NO postings? The generator would yield nothing for it \u2014 fine, no special coverage needed.\n\nNow, let me also think about whether `build_general_ledger` might be generic and need type params \u2014 no, Python generics are erased at runtime.\n\nEdge case test: empty journal and empty initial:\n\nNow let me also double check `JournalEntry(since, \"Sale on first day\", \"src\")` \u2014 the dataclass fields in order: `date`, `description`, `source`. So positional args: date, description, source. Good.\n\nAnd `.post(since, cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"250\")))` \u2014 signature `post(date, account, quantity)`. Good.\n\nOne thing: `Quantity(Decimal(\"250\"))` \u2014 NewType call at runtime is identity \u2192 `Decimal(\"250\")`. Good.\n\nNow the final code. Also, should I add `# noqa` or anything? No.\n\nShould I include a `conftest`-style fixture? No.\n\nLet me also reconsider: the task says \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\". My two tests do that. Also \"always making sure that each test is correct and indeed improves coverage\" \u2014 yes.\n\n\"Please also make VERY SURE to clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution; use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate.\" \u2014 The function under test is pure; no global state is touched; all objects are local to the test functions and garbage-collected. So no cleanup needed. I could add a brief comment noting this. I'll add a short comment.\n\nHmm, actually \u2014 is there ANY global state? `makeguid` might use a global counter or uuid \u2014 read-only usage. `JournalEntry` creation calls `makeguid` \u2014 no pollution. OK.\n\nLet me also double check the import of `Ledger` \u2014 used in `isinstance(cash_ledger, Ledger)`. Good, that also verifies the created objects are `Ledger` instances.\n\nWait, one more potential gotcha: `GeneralLedger` and `Ledger` \u2014 are they maybe re-exported from `pypara.accounting.ledger`? They're defined there per the snippet context (the snippet is from ledger.py and references them without import). Yes.\n\nAlso `InitialBalances` is defined in ledger.py per get_info (\"in pypara/accounting/ledger.py\"? \u2014 the get_info output for InitialBalances didn't specify the file, but it imports Account and Balance; the snippet uses `InitialBalances` as a type annotation in ledger.py, so it's defined/imported there). I don't need it.\n\nFinal check on assertion `gl.ledgers[equity].entries == []` \u2014 `Ledger.entries` is a list; `== []` \u2192 True if empty. Good.\n\nOne more: `assert set(gl.ledgers) == {cash, sales, inventory, equity}` \u2014 MockAccount is hashable (default object hash). Good.\n\nLet me write the final file now.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nTests for `pypara.accounting.ledger.build_general_ledger` covering lines 174-186.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport datetime\nfrom decimal import Decimal\n\nfrom pypara.accounting.generic import Balance\nfrom pypara.accounting.journaling import JournalEntry\nfrom pypara.accounting.ledger import GeneralLedger, Ledger, build_general_ledger\nfrom pypara.commons.numbers import Quantity\nfrom pypara.commons.zeitgeist import DateRange\n\n\nclass MockAccount:\n    \"\"\"\n    A minimal concrete account implementation satisfying the `Account` protocol.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, code, name):\n        self._code = code\n        self._name = name\n\n    @property\n    def code(self):\n        return self._code\n\n    @property\n    def name(self):\n        return self._name\n\n    @property\n    def type(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def coa(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def parent(self):\n        return None\n\n\ndef test_build_general_ledger_with_empty_journal_and_no_initial_balances():\n    \"\"\"\n    An empty journal and empty initial balances must produce an empty general ledger.\n    \"\"\"\n    period = DateRange(datetime.date(2022, 1, 1), datetime.date(2022, 12, 31))\n\n    gl = build_general_ledger(period, [], {})\n\n    assert isinstance(gl, GeneralLedger)\n    assert gl.period == period\n    assert gl.ledgers == {}\n\n\ndef test_build_general_ledger_populates_ledgers():\n    \"\"\"\n    Building a general ledger must create ledgers for accounts with initial balances,\n    create zero-initialised ledgers for accounts first seen in in-period postings,\n    add in-period postings to the ledgers of their accounts, and ignore journal\n    entries dated outside of the period.\n    \"\"\"\n    since = datetime.date(2021, 1, 1)\n    until = datetime.date(2021, 12, 31)\n    period = DateRange(since, until)\n\n    cash = MockAccount(\"100\", \"Cash\")\n    sales = MockAccount(\"400\", \"Sales\")\n    inventory = MockAccount(\"110\", \"Inventory\")\n    equity = MockAccount(\"300\", \"Equity\")  # initial balance, but never posted to\n    rent = MockAccount(\"500\", \"Rent Expense\")  # only posted to outside the period\n\n    initial = {\n        cash: Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(\"1000\"))),\n        equity: Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(\"5000\"))),\n    }\n\n    ## Journal entries within the period, including both boundary dates:\n    je_on_since = (\n        JournalEntry(since, \"Sale on first day\", \"src\")\n        .post(since, cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"250\")))\n        .post(since, sales, Quantity(Decimal(\"250\")))\n    )\n    je_mid_period = (\n        JournalEntry(datetime.date(2021, 6, 15), \"Inventory purchase\", \"src\")\n        .post(datetime.date(2021, 6, 15), inventory, Quantity(Decimal(\"40\")))\n        .post(datetime.date(2021, 6, 15), cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"75\")))\n    )\n    je_on_until = (\n        JournalEntry(until, \"Sale on last day\", \"src\")\n        .post(until, cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))\n    )\n\n    ## Journal entries outside the period; these must be ignored entirely:\n    je_before = (\n        JournalEntry(datetime.date(2020, 12, 31), \"Prior-year entry\", \"src\")\n        .post(datetime.date(2020, 12, 31), rent, Quantity(Decimal(\"999\")))\n        .post(datetime.date(2020, 12, 31), cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"999\")))\n    )\n    je_after = (\n        JournalEntry(datetime.date(2022, 1, 1), \"Next-year entry\", \"src\")\n        .post(datetime.date(2022, 1, 1), rent, Quantity(Decimal(\"888\")))\n    )\n\n    journal = [je_before, je_on_since, je_mid_period, je_on_until, je_after]\n\n    ## NOTE: `build_general_ledger` is a pure function; it allocates all of its state\n    ## locally and mutates no globals, hence no monkeypatching/cleanup is required here.\n    gl = build_general_ledger(period, journal, initial)\n\n    ## A general ledger over the requested period is returned:\n    assert isinstance(gl, GeneralLedger)\n    assert gl.period == period\n\n    ## Ledgers exist exactly for accounts with initial balances or in-period postings:\n    assert set(gl.ledgers) == {cash, sales, inventory, equity}\n    assert rent not in gl.ledgers\n\n    ## Ledgers seeded from initial balances keep those balances:\n    assert isinstance(gl.ledgers[cash], Ledger)\n    assert gl.ledgers[cash].account == cash\n    assert gl.ledgers[cash].initial == initial[cash]\n    assert gl.ledgers[equity].account == equity\n    assert gl.ledgers[equity].initial == initial[equity]\n    assert gl.ledgers[equity].entries == []\n\n    ## Ledgers created on the fly for accounts without initial balances start at zero\n    ## on the first day of the period:\n    assert gl.ledgers[sales].initial == Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))\n    assert gl.ledgers[inventory