ore, so I need to make a judgment call.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The safest assertions that don't depend on the exact arithmetic of `Ledger.add`:\n- Number of entries.\n- Entry dates.\n- That entries reference the correct journal.\n\nBut balance assertions are valuable. Let me think about what the actual pypara code does. From the pypara GitHub repository (serpapi/pypara? no, it's \"pypara\" by ... hmm). Let me recall: pypara is a double-entry accounting library by Onur Rauf Soy? Actually it's by \"kolanos\"? Hmm. I recall pypara is by \"Onur\" ... Actually pypara is https://github.com/pypara/pypara? Let me think. The package \"pypara\" on PyPI: \"pypara \u2014 Double-entry accounting library for Python\". Author: \"Onur Rauf\"? I don't remember.\n\nThe actual source of pypara/accounting/ledger.py:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nThis module provides classes for ledger and general ledger models.\n\"\"\"\nimport datetime\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom typing import Dict, Generic, Iterable, List, NewType, Tuple, Type, TypeVar\n\nfrom pypara.commons.numbers import Quantity, ZERO\nfrom pypara.commons.zeitgeist import DateRange\nfrom pypara.accounting.accounts import Account\nfrom pypara.accounting.generic import Balance\nfrom pypara.accounting.journaling import JournalEntry, Posting\n\n_T = TypeVar(\"_T\")\n\nInitialBalances = Dict[Account, Balance]\n\"\"\"\nType of initial balances.\n\"\"\"\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass LedgerEntry(Generic[_T]):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides a ledger entry model.\n    \"\"\"\n    ledger: \"Ledger[_T]\"\n    journal: JournalEntry[_T]\n    date: datetime.date\n    direction: Direction\n    quantity: Quantity\n    balance: Balance\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Ledger(Generic[_T]):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides an account ledger model.\n    \"\"\"\n    account: Account\n    initial: Balance\n    entries: List[LedgerEntry[_T]] = field(default_factory=list, init=False)\n\n    @property\n    def _last_balance(self) -> Quantity:\n        \"\"\"\n        Returns the last balance of this ledger.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self.entries[-1].balance.value if self.entries else self.initial.value\n\n    def add(self, posting: Posting[_T]) -> LedgerEntry[_T]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a posting to this ledger.\n        \"\"\"\n        balance: Balance = Balance(posting.date, self._last_balance + posting.quantity if posting.is_debit else self._last_balance - posting.quantity)\n        entry: LedgerEntry[_T] = LedgerEntry(self, posting.journal, posting.date, posting.direction, posting.quantity, balance)\n        self.entries.append(entry)\n        return entry\n```\n\nHmm wait, but `Posting` has `amount`, not `quantity`. Unless in this version `Posting` has `quantity`. The get_info output for Posting says:\n\n```python\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Posting(Generic[_T]):\n    journal: 'JournalEntry[_T]'\n    date: datetime.date\n    account: Account\n    direction: Direction\n    amount: Amount\n```\n\nSo `Posting` has `amount: Amount`. And `Amount` is likely `NewType('Amount', Decimal)`.\n\nHmm, so in `Ledger.add`, it probably uses `posting.amount`. And `JournalEntry.post(date, account, quantity)` \u2014 the quantity becomes the amount.\n\nOK here's the thing: I can't verify the exact internals anymore. Let me write assertions that are robust:\n\n1. Structural assertions (definitely safe):\n   - `isinstance(gl, GeneralLedger)`\n   - `gl.period == period`\n   - set of ledger keys\n   - `gl.ledgers[acct].initial` for both initial-provided and auto-created ledgers\n   - `len(gl.ledgers[acct].entries)`\n   - entry dates match posting dates\n\n2. Balance assertions (probably safe but depends on arithmetic):\n   - final balance = initial + sum of positive quantities\n\nI'll include balance assertions but make them consistent with the most likely implementation (INC/debit adds). Actually, hmm, let me think again about whether `post` with a positive quantity gives INC.\n\nIn pypara journali