ral_ledger` which is in ledger.py. So `Ledger` and `GeneralLedger` are both in ledger.py. Good.\n\nNow the mock account:\n\n```python\nclass MockAccount:\n    \"\"\"\n    A minimal concrete account implementation for testing.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, code: str, name: str):\n        self._code = code\n        self._name = name\n\n    @property\n    def code(self):\n        return self._code\n\n    @property\n    def name(self):\n        return self._name\n\n    @property\n    def type(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def coa(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def parent(self):\n        return None\n```\n\nHmm, `type` as a property shadows builtin `type` only within the class \u2014 fine.\n\nAlso, hashability: default object hash works. And equality: default identity equality \u2014 fine for dict keys.\n\nNow the test:\n\n```python\ndef test_build_general_ledger_full_coverage():\n    # Period\n    period = DateRange(datetime.date(2021, 1, 1), datetime.date(2021, 12, 31))\n\n    # Accounts\n    cash = MockAccount(\"100\", \"Cash\")\n    sales = MockAccount(\"400\", \"Sales\")\n    inventory = MockAccount(\"110\", \"Inventory\")\n\n    # Initial balances: cash has an opening balance; inventory does not.\n    initial = {\n        cash: Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(\"1000\"))),\n    }\n\n    # Journal entries:\n    # 1. Within period, touches cash (has initial) and sales (new ledger).\n    je1 = JournalEntry(datetime.date(2021, 6, 15), \"Sale\", None).post(\n        datetime.date(2021, 6, 15), cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"250\"))\n    ).post(\n        datetime.date(2021, 6, 15), sales, Quantity(Decimal(\"250\"))\n    )\n    ...\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `JournalEntry` is frozen dataclass; `post` mutates `self.postings` (a list) \u2014 that's allowed since the list itself is mutable. And `post` returns the entry for chaining. But careful: `JournalEntry(date, description, source)` \u2014 `source` is `_T`. I'll use `None` or a string as source.\n\nHmm wait, actually, does `post` return `self`? The signature says `-> 'JournalEntry[_T]'`, so yes it returns a JournalEntry \u2014 presumably self. Chaining should work.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 is `post` maybe creating a new entry? No, it appends to `self.postings` and returns self (that's the standard pypara implementation). OK.\n\n2. Journal entry outside period (before since): should be ignored.\n3. Journal entry outside period (after until): should be ignored.\n4. Journal entry within period touching inventory (account not in initial) \u2192 creates new ledger with zero initial balance.\n\nThen assertions:\n\n```python\n    gl = build_general_ledger(period, journal, initial)\n\n    assert isinstance(gl, GeneralLedger)\n    assert gl.period == period\n\n    # Ledgers exist for accounts with initial balances and in-period postings:\n    assert set(gl.ledgers.keys()) == {cash, sales, inventory}\n\n    # Ledger for account with initial balance keeps that balance:\n    assert gl.ledgers[cash].initial == initial[cash]\n    assert gl.ledgers[cash].account == cash\n\n    # Ledger for account without initial balance starts at zero on period start:\n    assert gl.ledgers[inventory].initial == Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))\n\n    # Postings within the period are added to the ledgers:\n    assert len(gl.ledgers[cash].entries) == 2  # two in-period postings on cash\n    assert len(gl.ledgers[sales].entries) == 1\n    assert len(gl.ledgers[inventory].entries) == 1\n\n    # Balance arithmetic: initial + increments\n    assert gl.ledgers[cash].entries[-1].balance.value == Decimal(\"1000\") + Decimal(\"250\") + Decimal(\"75\")\n    assert gl.ledgers[sales].entries[-1].balance.value == Decimal(\"250\")\n    assert gl.ledgers[inventory].entries[-1].balance.value == Decimal(\"40\")\n```\n\nWait, careful with the balance arithmetic. If cash has initial 1000, and two in-period postings of +250 and +75, final balance = 1325. But this assumes `is_debit` for INC adds. If the implementation subtracts for INC... that would be weird. I'm fairly confident INC adds.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 there's a subtlety with `sales`: sales is an income account; in double-entry, a sale credits sales (DEC). But for this test, direction semantics don't matter; I just need postings. Using positive quantities for all is fine.\n\nActually, hmm, let me reconsider: maybe I should make one posting negative to exercise the DEC path in `Ledger.add`? But that's not part of the uncovered lines (174-186) \u2014 the uncovered lines are only in `build_general_ledger`. The `Ledger.add` internals are presumably covered elsewhere (or not, but my task is lines 174-186). Still, exercising both directions doesn't hurt, but it complicates balance assertions. Let me keep it simple: all positive quantities. Actually, wait \u2014 using a negative quantity would exercise `Direction.DEC` in `post`, but again not needed for coverage of 174-186. Keep it simple and robust.\n\nHmm, but actually, let me reconsider whether to assert exact balance values at all. The risk: if `Ledger.add` computes balance differently (e.g., subtracts for INC), my test fails. But the task says tests must be \"correct\". Given my strong recollection of pypara's implementation, INC adds. Also, semantically, `Ledger.add` for a debit posting adds to the balance. I'll go with it.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the `_last_balance` property: `self.entries[-1].balance.value if self.entries else self.initial.value`. So for the cash ledger with initial 1000: first posting +250 \u2192 balance 1250; second +75 \u2192 1325. Good.\n\nFor sales (new ledger, initial 0): +250 \u2192 250. Good.\n\nFor inventory (new ledger, initial 0): +40 \u2192 40. Good.\n\nNow, about the out-of-period journal entries: I'll create entries dated before `period.since` and after `period.until`, with postings to a separate account (e.g., `old_account`), and assert that account is NOT in `gl.ledgers`. Also, an out-of-period posting to `cash` should not appear in cash's entries \u2014 I'll add an out-of-period posting to cash too, and assert cash's entries count is 2 (only in-period ones). That tests the filter condition thoroughly.\n\nWait, but careful: the filter is on `j.date` (the journal entry's date), not the posting's date. `period.since <= j.date <= period.until`. So a journal entry dated outside the period is entirely skipped, even if its postings have in-period dates. I'll create out-of-period entries with dates outside.\n\nAlso, boundary: a journal entry dated exactly on `period.since` and exactly on `period.until` should be included (inclusive bounds). That exercises the `<=` branches. Let me include entries on both boundary dates.\n\nLet me now also think about the `initial` dict: line 174 creates ledgers from initial balances. I'll have one account with an initial balance. Should I also test with an empty initial dict? That would still execute line 174 (with empty dict comprehension). The main test covers line 174 with a non-empty dict. Maybe add a second test with empty initial to cover the \"no initial balances\" path \u2014 but that's the same line. Not needed for coverage, but a separate test for the empty case is nice. Actually, the task says create tests that execute all missing lines and branches. Lines 174-186 are the whole function. Branches: the `if posting.account not in ledgers` has two branches (true \u2192 line 180, false \u2192 skip to 183). My test covers both: cash is in ledgers (from initial) \u2192 false branch; sales/inventory not in ledgers \u2192 true branch. The generator filter `if period.since <= j.date <= period.until` has true/false branches \u2014 covered by in-period and out-of-period entries.\n\nAlso, the case where journal is empty and initial is empty \u2192 returns empty GeneralLedger. Could add as a separate small test. That covers line 174 with empty dict, 177 with empty generator, 186. It's a nice edge case. I'll add it.\n\nNow, cleanup/state pollution: The test creates local objects only; no global state is modified. `JournalEntry` instances are local. No monkeypatching needed. But the task says \"make VERY SURE to clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution; use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate.\" Since we don't touch global state, nothing to clean up. I could note that. There's no file I/O or singleton state. So no cleanup needed \u2014 but I should make sure I don't create any module-level mutable state. I'll define everything inside test functions.\n\nWait \u2014 one thing: `makeguid` for `guid` field \u2014 uses uuid presumably, no global state.\n\nNow, imports: I need `build_general_ledger`, `GeneralLedger`, `Ledger` from `pypara.accounting.ledger`. Also `JournalEntry` from `pypara.accounting.journaling`, `Balance` from `pypara.accounting.generic`, `Quantity` from `pypara.commons.numbers`, `DateRange` from `pypara.commons.zeitgeist`.\n\nHmm, one concern: does `pypara.accounting.ledger` exist as a module with these names? The snippet says \"extracted from pypara/accounting/ledger.py\". Yes.\n\nAnother concern: `Quantity(Decimal(0))` \u2014 `Quantity` is a NewType, so `Quantity(Decimal(0))` is just `Decimal(0)`. And `Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))` equality with the auto-created one: `Balance` is frozen dataclass \u2192 structural equality. `Decimal(0) == Decimal(0)` \u2192 True. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 in my assertion `gl.ledgers[inventory].initial == Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))` \u2014 the auto-created initial is `Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))`. Equality: date equal, value equal (Decimal(0) == Decimal(0)). Good.\n\nHmm, one more: `Decimal(\"1000\") + Decimal(\"250\") + Decimal(\"75\")` = `Decimal(\"1325\")`. And `Decimal(\"1325\") == Decimal(\"1000\") + ...` \u2192 True. Good.\n\nNow, about `JournalEntry` construction: `JournalEntry(date, description, source)`. `source` is `_T` \u2014 I'll use a simple string like \"source\" or None. Fine.\n\nWait, is `postings` really excluded from init? `field(default_factory=list, init=False)` \u2014 yes. So constructor takes (date, description, source) positionally or by keyword. And `guid` is also init=False.\n\nLet me double check `post` chaining: `JournalEntry(...).post(d1, a1, q1).post(d2, a2, q2)` \u2014 each `post` returns the entry. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait: does `post` validate anything? Like requiring the date to match the journal date? In pypara:\n\n```python\n    def post(self, date: datetime.date, account: Account, quantity: Quantity) -> \"JournalEntry[_T]\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a new posting to this journal entry.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.postings.append(Posting(self, date, account, Direction.INC if quantity >= ZERO else Direction.DEC, quantity))\n        return self\n```\n\nI don't think it validates. `validate()` is separate. OK.\n\nNow, one more consideration: the `Posting` date vs journal date. In `build_general_ledger`, the filter uses `j.date`. The postings' own dates could differ, but typically they match. I'll make posting dates match journal dates.\n\nLet me also think: should I use `pytest` fixtures? Not necessary. Keep it simple with plain test functions.\n\nLet me also double-check the `MockAccount` \u2014 since `Account` is a `runtime_checkable` Protocol, and `build_general_ledger` doesn't do isinstance checks (it just uses accounts as dict keys), my mock is fine. But `Ledger` dataclass stores `account` \u2014 fine.\n\nActually, hmm, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe pypara's accounts.py has a concrete `Account` class too (the Protocol might be named `Account` and there might be an implementation). The get_info returned the Protocol for \"Account\". If there were a concrete class with the same name, get_info would presumably return one of them. To be safe, I'll use my own mock class. That's guaranteed to work.\n\nNow, let me also think about whether `build_general_ledger` might be generic and need type params \u2014 no, Python generics are erased at runtime.\n\nEdge case test: empty journal and empty initial:\n\n```python\ndef test_build_general_ledger_empty():\n    period = DateRange(datetime.date(2022, 1, 1), datetime.date(2022, 12, 31))\n    gl = build_general_ledger(period, [], {})\n    assert isinstance(gl, GeneralLedger)\n    assert gl.period == period\n    assert gl.ledgers == {}\n```\n\nGood.\n\nMain test:\n\n```python\ndef test_build_general_ledger_populates_ledgers():\n    since = datetime.date(2021, 1, 1)\n    until = datetime.date(2021, 12, 31)\n    period = DateRange(since, until)\n\n    cash = MockAccount(\"100\", \"Cash\")\n    sales = MockAccount(\"400\", \"Sales\")\n    inventory = MockAccount(\"110\", \"Inventory\")\n    rent = MockAccount(\"500\", \"Rent Expense\")  # only has out-of-period postings\n\n    initial = {cash: Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(\"1000\")))}\n\n    # In-period entries:\n    je_since = (\n        JournalEntry(since, \"Opening sale\", \"src\")\n        .post(since, cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"250\")))\n        .post(since, sales, Quantity(Decimal(\"250\")))\n    )\n    je_mid = (\n        JournalEntry(datetime.date(2021, 6, 15), \"Inventory purchase\", \"src\")\n        .post(datetime.date(2021, 6, 15), inventory, Quantity(Decimal(\"40\")))\n        .post(datetime.date(2021, 6, 15), cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"75\")))\n    )\n    je_until = (\n        JournalEntry(until, \"Year-end sale\", \"src\")\n        .post(until, cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))\n    )\n\n    # Out-of-period entries (must be ignored):\n    je_before = (\n        JournalEntry(datetime.date(2020, 12, 31), \"Prior-year entry\", \"src\")\n        .post(datetime.date(2020, 12, 31), rent, Quantity(Decimal(\"999\")))\n        .post(datetime.date(2020, 12, 31), cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"999\")))\n    )\n    je_after = (\n        JournalEntry(datetime.date(2022, 1, 1), \"Next-year entry\", \"src\")\n        .post(datetime.date(2022, 1, 1), rent, Quantity(Decimal(\"888\")))\n    )\n\n    journal = [je_before, je_since, je_mid, je_until, je_after]\n\n    gl = build_general_ledger(period, journal, initial)\n\n    assert isinstance(gl, GeneralLedger)\n    assert gl.period == period\n\n    # Only accounts with initial balances or in-period postings get ledgers:\n    assert set(gl.ledgers) == {cash, sales, inventory}\n    assert rent not in gl.ledgers\n\n    # Ledger for the account with an initial balance:\n    cash_ledger = gl.ledgers[cash]\n    assert isinstance(cash_ledger, Ledger)\n    assert cash_ledger.account == cash\n    assert cash_ledger.initial == initial[cash]\n    assert len(cash_ledger.entries) == 3  # je_since, je_mid, je_until postings\n    assert cash_ledger.entries[-1].balance.value == Decimal(\"1000\") + Decimal(\"250\") + Decimal(\"75\") + Decimal(\"100\")\n\n    # Ledger auto-created for an account without an initial balance:\n    sales_ledger = gl.ledgers[sales]\n    assert sales_ledger.account == sales\n    assert sales_ledger.initial == Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))\n    assert len(sales_ledger.entries) == 1\n    assert sales_ledger.entries[-1].balance.value == Decimal(\"250\")\n\n    inventory_ledger = gl.ledgers[inventory]\n    assert inventory_ledger.initial == Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))\n    assert len(inventory_ledger.entries) == 1\n    assert inventory_ledger.entries[-1].balance.value == Decimal(\"40\")\n```\n\nWait, cash entries: je_since posts cash +250 (1 entry), je_mid posts cash +75 (1 entry), je_until posts cash +100 (1 entry). Total 3 entries. Final balance = 1000 + 250 + 75 + 100 = 1425. Good.\n\nHmm, but the order of entries in cash ledger: postings are processed in journal iteration order: je_before (skipped), je_since (cash +250), je_mid (cash +75), je_until (cash +100), je_after (skipped). So cash entries: [250-entry, 75-entry, 100-entry]. Final balance 1425. Good.\n\nAlso, should I assert entry dates? `cash_ledger.entries[0].date == since` etc. I'll add a couple of date assertions \u2014 moderately safe. Actually, let me think once more about `LedgerEntry` fields. From `_last_balance`: `self.entries[-1].balance.value` \u2014 so `.balance.value` exists. For `.date`, I'm quite confident based on pypara's LedgerEntry definition. I'll include one date assertion. Hmm, risk vs reward... The task emphasizes correctness of tests. If `.date` doesn't exist, the test errors. I recall pypara's LedgerEntry:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass LedgerEntry(Generic[_T]):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides a ledger entry model.\n    \"\"\"\n    ledger: \"Ledger[_T]\"\n    journal: JournalEntry[_T]\n    date: datetime.date\n    direction: Direction\n    quantity: Quantity\n    balance: Balance\n```\n\nYes, I'm confident `date` exists. I'll include it but keep it minimal.\n\nActually, you know, let me drop the `.date` assertion to minimize risk, and keep count + balance assertions which are the core postconditions. Hmm. But dates verify the right postings were added... The count + balance already verify that effectively (out-of-period postings would change both count and balance). I'll skip `.date` to be safe. Actually no wait \u2014 let me include it. If I'm wrong about `.date`, the test fails and that's bad. If I'm right, it adds value. My confidence in `LedgerEntry.date` is high (~90%). Hmm. The balance assertions already catch out-of-period leakage (999 and 888 would show up in balances). Count assertions catch it too. I'll skip `.date` \u2014 safer. Actually... let me include a check on `entries[-1].balance.date`? `Balance` has `date` and `value` \u2014 that's confirmed from get_info. `entries[-1].balance.date == until` for cash. That's safe (Balance structure confirmed). I'll use that instead of LedgerEntry.date. \n\nHmm wait, is `balance.date` the posting date? `Balance(posting.date, ...)` \u2014 yes, the balance is dated at the posting date. So `cash_ledger.entries[-1].balance.date == until`. Safe and meaningful. \n\nNow, about `Quantity` comparisons: `Quantity(Decimal(\"1000\"))` is `Decimal(\"1000\")`. `cash_ledger.initial == initial[cash]` \u2192 Balance equality \u2192 `Decimal(\"1000\") == Decimal(\"1000\")` \u2192 True. Good.\n\nOne more thing: `assert set(gl.ledgers) == {cash, sales, inventory}` \u2014 set of keys uses hash/eq of MockAccount instances \u2014 identity-based, fine since same instances.\n\nNow, about the `source` field: `JournalEntry(date, description, source)`. I'll pass a string like \"test-source\". Fine.\n\nAlso \u2014 potential issue: `JournalEntry` is frozen; `post` appends to `self.postings`. If `post` internally does `self.postings.append(...)`, that's fine with frozen (frozen only blocks `self.postings = ...`).\n\nHmm, wait, actually there's a subtle issue: frozen dataclasses use `object.__setattr__` for init, and `postings` is initialized via default_factory. Appending to the list is fine.\n\nNow, imports \u2014 do I need `pytest` at all? I'm not using fixtures or monkeypatch since there's no global state. The task says \"use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate\" \u2014 it's not appropriate here since the function is pure (no global state, no I/O). I'll not import pytest... Actually, maybe I should still import pytest? No \u2014 unused imports are fine but unnecessary. I'll skip importing pytest since no pytest APIs are used. Hmm, but the task says \"Write as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 plain test functions are fine.\n\nWait, actually, should I worry about `Ledger` import \u2014 is `Ledger` exported from `pypara.accounting.ledger`? Yes, it's defined there (the snippet's module). And `GeneralLedger` too. And `InitialBalances` (a type alias) \u2014 I don't need to import it.\n\nLet me also double check: `from pypara.commons.numbers import Quantity` \u2014 confirmed by get_info. `from pypara.commons.zeitgeist import DateRange` \u2014 confirmed. `from pypara.accounting.generic import Balance` \u2014 confirmed. `from pypara.accounting.journaling import JournalEntry` \u2014 confirmed.\n\nOne more consideration: the `MockAccount` needs `code`, `name`, `type`, `coa`, `parent` properties to satisfy the Protocol if anything does isinstance checks. `runtime_checkable` protocols with non-method members: isinstance checks only verify presence of attributes (for data protocols, isinstance checks attribute presence via hasattr). My mock has all as properties. Good.\n\nHmm, wait: `runtime_checkable` protocols with non-callable members \u2014 `isinstance()` checks work via hasattr for data protocols. My mock has all attributes. Fine.\n\nNow, is there any risk that `Ledger.add` fails with my postings? `Ledger.add(posting)` uses `posting.is_debit`, `posting.amount` (or quantity), `posting.journal`, `posting.date`, `posting.direction`. All present via `post()`. Good.\n\nWhat about `Quantity` type \u2014 `post` expects `Quantity`; I pass `Quantity(Decimal(\"250\"))` which is `Decimal(\"250\")`. Fine.\n\nLet me also consider: should the second test (empty) also assert `gl.ledgers == {}`? Yes.\n\nAlso, maybe a test where `initial` contains an account that has NO postings \u2014 the ledger exists with zero entries. That's covered by... hmm, in my main test, all initial accounts have postings. Let me add an account with an initial balance but no postings, and assert its ledger exists with empty entries and its initial balance preserved. That exercises line 174 more thoroughly (ledger created from initial, never touched by postings). Let me add `equity = MockAccount(\"300\", \"Equity\")` with initial balance, no postings. Assert `gl.ledgers[equity].entries == []` and initial matches.\n\nWait, but then `set(gl.ledgers)` = {cash, sales, inventory, equity}. Good.\n\nHmm, one thing to double check: `Ledger.entries` default is `field(default_factory=list, init=False)` \u2014 so `entries == []` for untouched ledger. Good.\n\nNow let me also think about whether `build_general_ledger` might be generic and need type params \u2014 no, Python generics are erased at runtime.\n\nEdge case test: empty journal and empty initial:\n\nNow, the out-of-period entries: `je_before` dated 2020-12-31 (before since 2021-01-01) and `je_after` dated 2022-01-01 (after until 2021-12-31). Both skipped. The `rent` account only appears in out-of-period entries \u2192 not in ledgers. Also cash gets an out-of-period posting of 999 \u2014 if the filter were broken, cash's balance would include it; my assertions catch that.\n\nBoundary entries: je_since dated exactly `since`, je_until dated exactly `until` \u2014 both included (tests `<=` on both ends).\n\nNow, let me write the final test file.\n\n```python\nimport datetime\nfrom decimal import Decimal\n\nfrom pypara.accounting.generic import Balance\nfrom pypara.accounting.journaling import JournalEntry\nfrom pypara.accounting.ledger import GeneralLedger, Ledger, build_general_ledger\nfrom pypara.commons.numbers import Quantity\nfrom pypara.commons.zeitgeist import DateRange\n\n\nclass MockAccount:\n    \"\"\"\n    A minimal concrete account implementation used for testing purposes.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, code: str, name: str):\n        self._code = code\n        self._name = name\n\n    @property\n    def code(self) -> str:\n        return self._code\n\n    @property\n    def name(self) -> str:\n        return self._name\n\n    @property\n    def type(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def coa(self):\n        return None\n\n    @property\n    def parent(self):\n        return None\n\n\ndef test_build_general_ledger_empty_journal_and_initials():\n    \"\"\"\n    Building a general ledger over an empty journal with no initial balances\n    must yield an empty general ledger for the given period.\n    \"\"\"\n    period = DateRange(datetime.date(2022, 1, 1), datetime.date(2022, 12, 31))\n\n    gl = build_general_ledger(period, [], {})\n\n    assert isinstance(gl, GeneralLedger)\n    assert gl.period == period\n    assert gl.ledgers == {}\n\n\ndef test_build_general_ledger_populates_ledgers():\n    \"\"\"\n    Building a general ledger must:\n    * create ledgers for all accounts with initial balances,\n    * create ledgers, with zero opening balances, for accounts posted to within the period,\n    * ignore journal entries dated outside of the period,\n    * add in-period postings to the ledgers of their accounts.\n    \"\"\"\n    since = datetime.date(2021, 1, 1)\n    until = datetime.date(2021, 12, 31)\n    period = DateRange(since, until)\n\n    cash = MockAccount(\"100\", \"Cash\")\n    sales = MockAccount(\"400\", \"Sales\")\n    inventory = MockAccount(\"110\", \"Inventory\")\n    equity = MockAccount(\"300\", \"Equity\")  # has an initial balance, but no postings\n    rent = MockAccount(\"500\", \"Rent Expense\")  # only posted to outside of the period\n\n    initial = {\n        cash: Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(\"1000\"))),\n        equity: Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(\"5000\"))),\n    }\n\n    ## Journal entries within the period (including both boundary dates):\n    je_on_since = (\n        JournalEntry(since, \"Sale on first day\", \"src\")\n        .post(since, cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"250\")))\n        .post(since, sales, Quantity(Decimal(\"250\")))\n    )\n    je_mid = (\n        JournalEntry(datetime.date(2021, 6, 15), \"Inventory purchase\", \"src\")\n        .post(datetime.date(2021, 6, 15), inventory, Quantity(Decimal(\"40\")))\n        .post(datetime.date(2021, 6, 15), cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"75\")))\n    )\n    je_on_until = (\n        JournalEntry(until, \"Sale on last day\", \"src\")\n        .post(until, cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"100\")))\n    )\n\n    ## Journal entries outside of the period (must be ignored entirely):\n    je_before = (\n        JournalEntry(datetime.date(2020, 12, 31), \"Prior-year entry\", \"src\")\n        .post(datetime.date(2020, 12, 31), rent, Quantity(Decimal(\"999\")))\n        .post(datetime.date(2020, 12, 31), cash, Quantity(Decimal(\"999\")))\n    )\n    je_after = (\n        JournalEntry(datetime.date(2022, 1, 1), \"Next-year entry\", \"src\")\n        .post(datetime.date(2022, 1, 1), rent, Quantity(Decimal(\"888\")))\n    )\n\n    journal = [je_before, je_on_since, je_mid, je_on_until, je_after]\n\n    gl = build_general_ledger(period, journal, initial)\n\n    ## The result is a general ledger over the requested period:\n    assert isinstance(gl, GeneralLedger)\n    assert gl.period == period\n\n    ## Ledgers exist exactly for accounts with initial balances or in-period postings:\n    assert set(gl.ledgers) == {cash, sales, inventory, equity}\n    assert rent not in gl.ledgers\n\n    ## Ledgers of accounts with initial balances preserve those balances:\n    assert gl.ledgers[cash].account == cash\n    assert gl.ledgers[cash].initial == initial[cash]\n    assert gl.ledgers[equity].account == equity\n    assert gl.ledgers[equity].initial == initial[equity]\n    assert gl.ledgers[equity].entries == []\n\n    ## Ledgers of accounts without initial balances start at zero on the first day of the period:\n    assert gl.ledgers[sales].initial == Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))\n    assert gl.ledgers[inventory].initial == Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))\n\n    ## In-period postings are added to the ledgers of their accounts:\n    cash_ledger = gl.ledgers[cash]\n    assert isinstance(cash_ledger, Ledger)\n    assert len(cash_ledger.entries) == 3\n    assert cash_ledger.entries[-1].balance.date == until\n    assert cash_ledger.entries[-1].balance.value == Decimal(\"1000\") + Decimal(\"250\") + Decimal(\"75\") + Decimal(\"100\")\n\n    sales_ledger = gl.ledgers[sales]\n    assert len(sales_ledger.entries) == 1\n    assert sales_ledger.entries[-1].balance.value == Decimal(\"250\")\n\n    inventory_ledger = gl.ledgers[inventory]\n    assert len(inventory_ledger.entries) == 1\n    assert inventory_ledger.entries[-1].balance.value == Decimal(\"40\")\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `assert gl.ledgers[equity].entries == []` \u2014 entries is a list; comparing to [] works.\n\nHmm, one concern: `cash_ledger.entries[-1].balance.date == until` \u2014 the last cash posting is from je_on_until dated `until`. Balance date = posting date = until. Good.\n\nAnother concern: `Decimal(\"1000\") + Decimal(\"250\") + Decimal(\"75\") + Decimal(\"100\")` = Decimal(\"1425\"). And `entries[-1].balance.value` would be `Decimal(\"1425\")`. Equality holds.\n\nBut wait \u2014 what if `Ledger.add` stores balance as `Quantity(...)` which is just Decimal \u2014 equality fine.\n\nNow, potential issue: what if `Ledger.add` uses `posting.quantity` but `Posting` has `amount`? Then `Ledger.add` would fail with AttributeError \u2014 but that would be a bug in pypara itself, not my test. The get_info shows `Posting.amount`, so `Ledger.add` must use `posting.amount`. Fine either way \u2014 my test doesn't reference posting fields directly.\n\nAnother potential issue: `JournalEntry.post` \u2014 what if it doesn't return self? The signature says it returns `JournalEntry[_T]`. If it returned something else (like None), chaining would break. The type annotation strongly implies it returns the entry (self). I'm confident.\n\nHmm, but what if `post` requires the date to equal the journal's date or does validation? Unlikely. OK.\n\nOne more consideration: the `source` argument \u2014 `JournalEntry(date, description, source)`. Positional order: `date: datetime.date`, `description: str`, `source: _T`. Yes.\n\nNow, about state pollution: everything is local. No monkeypatch needed. The task says to clean up \u2014 since the function under test is pure (builds and returns new objects), no cleanup is required. I won't add unnecessary monkeypatching.\n\nShould I add a test using an iterator (generator) for journal instead of a list? The signature says `Iterable[JournalEntry]`. A list is an Iterable. Fine.\n\nAlso, should I test that the same account appearing in `initial` AND having postings reuses the existing ledger (line 179 false branch \u2192 line 183)? Yes \u2014 cash is in initial and has postings; `len(cash_ledger.entries) == 3` proves postings were added to the initial-balance ledger (not a fresh zero one). Good \u2014 that covers the false branch of the `if`.\n\nAnd sales/inventory cover the true branch (line 180).\n\nLine 174: covered by both tests (non-empty and empty initial).\nLine 177: covered (both with and without postings passing the filter).\nLine 179-180: covered (true branch).\nLine 183: covered.\nLine 186: covered.\n\nAll lines 174-186 covered. \n\nOne more thought: should I worry that `build_general_ledger` is in `pypara.accounting.ledger` but maybe `GeneralLedger`/`Ledger` need to be imported from there \u2014 yes, as discussed.\n\nAlso, is there any chance the module `pypara.accounting.ledger` imports fail due to missing dependencies? Not my concern.\n\nLet me also double-check `Quantity(Decimal(0))` vs `Decimal(0)`: `Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))` \u2014 `Quantity` is NewType, calling it returns the Decimal unchanged. The auto-created initial in the code is `Balance(period.since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))` \u2192 `Balance(since, Decimal(0))`. My expected: `Balance(since, Quantity(Decimal(0)))` \u2192 `Balance(since, Decimal(0))`. Equal. Good.\n\nHmm, `Decimal(0)` vs `Decimal(\"0\")` \u2014 equal values, and dataclass equality uses `==`, so `Decimal(0) == Decimal(\"0\")` \u2192 True. Good.\n\nNow, one subtle thing: in `test_build_general_ledger_empty_journal_and_initials`, passing `[]` as journal \u2014 the generator expression `(p for j in journal for p in j.postings if ...)` over empty list yields nothing. Good.\n\nLet me also consider adding a test where an account has an initial balance AND the journal is a generator (not list) \u2014 not needed.\n\nI think two tests suffice. Let me also consider: do I need `pytest` import for anything? No. But some style guides want it... I'll omit it. Actually, hmm \u2014 the instructions say \"Respond ONLY with the Python code enclosed in backti